1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,160
One is to settle upon a metric of success in advance.

2
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:10,140
Ooh, that's a really controversial one, right?

3
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,980
Because what happens if you wait for events to occur

4
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,420
before you announce the metric for success?

5
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:20,220
What do you think normal, weak human beings do?

6
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:23,560
Interpret whatever occurred as proof

7
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,320
of what it was that they believed.

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,560
And actually, people are even worse than this.

9
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:32,860
Often people will, if they make a mistake in their prediction

10
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:35,000
they will simply conveniently misremember

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,800
being right all along.

12
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,380
And that's where you'd want to have documentation

13
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:41,740
saying this is what you said, signed by you.

14
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:44,480
See, like, oh yeah, I guess I said

15
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:48,140
apartheid would last forever.

16
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,480
Well, all right.

17
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:54,320
And so, you know, Ted Locke has one book,

18
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:57,360
Expert Political Judgment, where he goes over

19
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,660
the big tournament among political experts

20
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:03,060
and shows not just how they did quite poorly,

21
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:06,400
but what's really striking is how people cope

22
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,400
with their own failures.

23
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,300
Because, you know, essentially there's almost no

24
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:14,140
intellectually honest coping that you can find in the data.

25
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:18,300
So, an expert on South Africa says apartheid will

26
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:21,820
last forever and then three years later it ends.

27
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:24,320
All right, so what do the experts say?

28
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:27,520
The first thing is he just misremembers his own prediction,

29
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:29,020
so that he was right all along.

30
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:31,160
And when you cut that away from him,

31
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:33,660
then, you know, like next stage is to say,

32
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:36,160
well, I was almost right.

33
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:38,200
I was almost right, you know, like, you know, like, right.

34
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:39,600
And then sometimes they'll say, well, you just can't

35
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,160
predict these matters anyway.

36
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:44,100
It's like, well, why didn't you say it was unpredictable

37
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:46,000
when you made the prediction?

38
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,800
Why do you wait until you have failed

39
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:50,900
and then start saying that predictions aren't possible?

40
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,580
Maybe they just aren't possible for you.

41
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:55,840
Maybe that, isn't that a better story

42
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:58,080
that you are a sore loser?

43
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,620
Right, now, Tadlock is so nice to them.

44
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:03,480
And also, he was all, it was all conditional on anonymity,

45
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,660
so he never outed anyone as a giant fraud.

46
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:08,120
You know, even though plainly, they're,

47
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:09,720
now they're the only, there were a few people

48
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:11,600
that were not in the tournament who just publicly

49
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,160
said ridiculous stuff, like Henry Kissinger, right?

50
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,000
A guy who, as far as I can tell, is wrong about

51
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,040
almost everything he's ever said in his entire life,

52
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:25,740
yet is considered a genius, a Machiavellian strategist

53
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:27,540
of the highest order.

54
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:28,840
It's like, you know, like, you were,

55
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,180
the North Vietnamese crushed you, like, what else,

56
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:33,320
what else, what's your other claim to fame here?

57
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:36,220
It's like, well, I have a very progressive accent.

58
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:38,620
You know, I sound really smart when I talk about things,

59
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:41,560
and I talk about this and mumble a bit, and, right?

60
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:44,300
So, anyway, Henry Kissinger, when he was asked

61
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,360
about his failure to break the end of the Cold War,

62
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:50,760
which he had many times given very strong predictions,

63
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:52,960
like there's no hope, and then he just said,

64
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:56,700
well, their crystal ball worked better than mine, ha ha.

65
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:02,600
So, what you're saying is, what, you are like an astrologer,

66
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:05,140
and the other astrologers got lucky,

67
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:07,820
but we shouldn't judge you negatively

68
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:11,560
because you're not worse than they are?

69
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:15,800
What is the theory here, is like, say, like,

70
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,380
that seems to be comparing himself to an astrologer,

71
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:20,480
which suggests to me that he is scum,

72
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:24,120
and should not be repeated by anyone,

73
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:28,200
and just be driven out, tarred and feathered,

74
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,460
and expelled from civilized discussion.

75
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:34,500
But, of course, that did not happen, right?

76
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:36,880
So, first of all, misremembering,

77
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:39,540
and then, I was almost right, I was so close,

78
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:42,440
or predictions are possible anyway, right?

79
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,080
And then there's also, like this one is one of the sleaziest,

80
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:48,520
say, look, sure I was wrong, but I made the right mistake.

81
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:51,080
And what does that even mean?

82
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:54,760
Basically, it means that you should be really risk averse,

83
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:58,360
and so I'm like the boy who cries wolf,

84
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:02,000
and that's good to be like the boy who cries wolf,

85
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,960
because maybe a wolf will come one day.

86
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,280
And that's what these people have,

87
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:08,600
and it's like, what?

88
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:14,240
That, you know, so, you know, like out of people

89
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:16,880
who predicted that there would be a horrible,

90
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:19,320
you know, like horrible terrorism, or a horrible war,

91
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:21,880
or genocide, or whatever, and then it doesn't happen.

92
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:24,200
Quite a few just say, look, fine, I was wrong,

93
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,740
but we should always plan for the worst, right?

94
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:32,540
So, basically, you're admitting that you were lying to us.

95
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:34,560
And why?

96
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:37,260
Because you think that if you tell the truth,

97
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:38,960
then people will say, that's low probability,

98
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:40,960
it's not worth many resources,

99
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:43,480
which is, in fact, the reasonable thing to do, generally,

100
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:45,720
is not to put a ton of resources into things

101
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:47,680
that are really bad, but hardly ever happen.

102
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:49,480
Certainly, if you've got things that are really bad,

103
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,720
and happen a lot, and other things that are really bad,

104
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:54,960
and hardly ever happen, which should get more resources,

105
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,080
things that are really bad, and happen a lot.

106
00:04:57,080 --> 00:04:57,920
Yes, please.

107
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:00,320
I just wanted to point out, and then, later in the book,

108
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:03,020
one, experts who got things right,

109
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:05,880
they never give people who got things wrong any of the,

110
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:08,400
Oh, yes, yes, yes, yes, also good, yes.

111
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:10,120
So, the people were right, you know,

112
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:11,720
like they claim it's scale.

113
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:15,080
So, if I'm right, it's scale, if I'm wrong, it's bad luck.

114
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:17,680
So, again, suggesting even the ones who got it right,

115
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:20,720
there's something very fishy about these people.

116
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:22,840
All right, now, the main thing you can say

117
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:26,240
in favor of Tetlock's experts is that there is that

118
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,480
he only asked questions that were controversial

119
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:31,360
in their experts, and so, it really wasn't possible

120
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:34,040
for the average expert to do well in his study.

121
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:35,560
So, and he explicitly said, like,

122
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:36,640
I've never asked questions like,

123
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:38,800
will there be a war between Norway and Sweden?

124
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:40,580
So, when people summarize it saying,

125
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:42,440
experts don't know more than regular people,

126
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:44,080
no, they do, they know more about

127
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:46,160
the uncontroversial stuff.

128
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:48,520
But, of course, experts have a lot more fun

129
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:50,880
arguing about the controversial stuff.

130
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,000
That's more likely, probably more likely to be televised,

131
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:54,240
but the debates are real.

132
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:58,080
All right, and then Tetlock has his follow-up book,

133
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:01,080
Superforecasting, where he runs huge tournaments

134
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:03,040
trying to find people who are able to do

135
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:05,200
what his experts were not able to do,

136
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,520
which is accurately predict the future.

137
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:10,960
And furthermore, he goes and does the obvious test

138
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:12,520
to make sure those people didn't just get lucky

139
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:15,200
by rerunning tournaments and seeing if the people

140
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:17,040
that did really well the first time

141
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:18,920
do above average the second time,

142
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,120
and with many different experimental variations,

143
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:25,160
and yes, there are actually people who can break the future,

144
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:27,200
and they have a number of interesting traits,

145
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:28,800
which we can all try to emulate.

146
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,920
But basically, it starts with being the opposite

147
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:34,640
of those experts I just described.

148
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:39,880
All right, okay, so questions about this?

149
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:42,280
Everyone ready to repent and live a totally new kind of life?

150
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:46,560
Yeah, so, Tetlock himself is so modest

151
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:48,360
and such a nice Canadian guy,

152
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:51,120
and I'm kind of like the apostle,

153
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:53,160
like just berating people for like,

154
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:55,160
the prophet has told us how we must live,

155
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:57,840
and you are sitting against the vain Tetlock, right?

156
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:00,600
And Tetlock's like, oh, all right.

157
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:05,600
But really, he just, after reading him,

158
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:08,200
people are so disappointing.

159
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:11,440
But this is inspirational.

160
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:13,960
I don't wanna be like regular people anymore.

161
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,280
I wanna be like, I wanna be a super forecaster,

162
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:17,320
and how can I do it?

163
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:21,800
All right, now, more general terms,

164
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:24,160
there's some evidence of systematic overconfidence.

165
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,680
So again, the simplest way of testing for overconfidence

166
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,400
is just to graph, say,

167
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:31,480
let's suppose this is a true-false question,

168
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:35,320
so it only makes sense to go and show probability

169
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:38,200
being 0% or 100% because you thought the probability

170
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:39,520
you're right was less than 50%,

171
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,680
you'd give the definite, you'd flip your answer, right?

172
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:46,480
All right, and so this here is the probability

173
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:50,480
that this is the actual percentage correct.

174
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:56,760
So this is like actual likelihood that you're right,

175
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,320
and this here is the perceived probability

176
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:00,800
that you're correct, right?

177
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,160
So if you're on the 45-degree line,

178
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:05,240
then this means that you've got

179
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:06,320
what's called perfect calibration,

180
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:08,720
where if you say 90% chance,

181
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:12,280
then you've got a 90% chance, okay?

182
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:15,280
All right, now, in practice, as you might guess,

183
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:16,800
people are quite a bit less likely

184
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,520
to be correct than they perceive, right?

185
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:22,440
So if, say, you say 60% chance of being right,

186
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:24,560
then you are going, you know,

187
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:26,560
then it's gonna be more like 55,

188
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,040
if I say, let's say, 60,

189
00:08:28,040 --> 00:08:29,960
and, you know, like, if you say 100,

190
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:31,760
then, again, like, usually you're probably

191
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:33,880
only like 80% right if you say 100,

192
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:36,120
so again, like, if the true line looks like that,

193
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:38,360
so this is the 45-degree line,

194
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:42,760
and this is, like, the actual line, all right?

195
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:44,920
So in that case, this would indicate overconfidence, right,

196
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:46,640
where you think that you are right

197
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:48,320
with a greater probability than you, in fact,

198
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:50,440
objectively are, right?

199
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:52,720
Okay, all right, so, you know,

200
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:54,280
this is pretty easy to find

201
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:57,440
if you go and ask people question by question

202
00:08:57,440 --> 00:08:59,200
how well they're doing,

203
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,120
although this is one of the results

204
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:02,440
that is more sensitive to the way

205
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:03,840
that you ask the question,

206
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:06,320
so if you change the question around and say,

207
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:07,920
like, how many questions total do you think

208
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:08,760
are right in this exam?

209
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:09,920
There's quite a bit less evidence

210
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:11,720
that people are overconfident,

211
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:14,680
so there's a German psychologist, Gerhard Gigerentzer,

212
00:09:14,680 --> 00:09:17,680
that has spent a lot of his time just saying

213
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:19,760
that the results that behavioral economists

214
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:24,760
get excited about are, in fact, overstated, right?

215
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:27,960
But, you know, there are, you know,

216
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:30,600
clear cases of overconfidence on things

217
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:33,520
that are very close to people's self-image.

218
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:35,840
For example, there's tests on, you know,

219
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:37,200
asking professors, do you think that you're

220
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:38,200
more productive in the media

221
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:39,920
than the rest of your department?

222
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:41,680
And, yeah, what do you think?

223
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:44,120
Almost all professors say, yeah,

224
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:45,840
they have, like, 90% claim to be more productive

225
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:47,760
than the media in their department.

226
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:49,400
Similarly, with, like, compared to driving ability

227
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:51,360
of the media, overwhelming majority of people

228
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:53,080
think that driving ability is better.

229
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:55,240
If there's one study of high school kids

230
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:57,800
that asked the 2,000 kids, like, you know,

231
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:59,560
would you say that you are better, average,

232
00:09:59,560 --> 00:10:03,480
or worse than average at relating socially with others?

233
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:04,960
And they got 2,000 kids surveyed,

234
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:08,320
and zero said they were worse than the average kid.

235
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:10,640
Which, you know, I think it means I was not in the survey.

236
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:13,440
I'm pretty sure I would have said I was worse.

237
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:15,160
But I don't know.

238
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:18,000
But anyway, so there's that.

239
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,520
Okay.

240
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:23,520
All right, so, questions about this stuff?

241
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:24,720
Happy?

242
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:27,760
All right, so why don't we go and take a break now.

243
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:32,560
Please, back to work, please.

244
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:36,560
All right.

245
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:39,160
All right.

246
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:41,360
So now we will continue our journey

247
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:44,160
through people's cognitive failings

248
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:46,760
when we are ourselves somewhat tired.

249
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:50,560
But does an economist care about being tired?

250
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:53,160
No!

251
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:54,560
We are not daunted by this.

252
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:56,360
We are not subject to these normal constraints.

253
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:59,360
And so we will proceed at the full height of our abilities

254
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:01,560
as we look down upon the failures of others.

255
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:02,960
All right.

256
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:04,360
All right.

257
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:07,560
So, availability and representative-ist biases,

258
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:10,760
